{"id":"consistently-faster","title":"Consistently faster, like for like","question":"Which firms and licensed professionals get building permits faster than like permits, year after year?","finding":"60 of 1,157 firms and professionals tested meet the test for consistently faster building permits, where chance alone would produce about 0.05; 37 of them still stand out after also matching the kind of work, and 11 do more than half their jobs in a single kind of work. At the usual one-in-twenty threshold 517 firms differ from like permits against about 58 expected by luck, so who files a permit is strongly tied to how fast it is issued. Strongest after the control: E. J. Weber Elect.Co Inc, faster on 88% of 99 jobs (2015–2024), a median 0 days against 10 for like permits. 9 further individuals who are neither licensed nor registered meet the test and are counted, not named.","how":"Every issued building permit with drawings filed 2012 to 2024 (179,746 permits) was ranked by days from filing to issue among like permits: the same permit type, review path (counter or full review, Fire-only, site permit, online filing, and the department's own priority flag), cost band and filing year. Cells under 30 permits were merged upward (three-year period, then paired cost bands) and 388 permits with no cell of 30 were dropped. A job is faster when it beat its cell's median; ties at the median are split in proportion so that chance gives exactly one half. Firms are tested with 40 or more jobs and at least 3 active years (5 or more jobs in the year); jobs for one lot filed on one day count once. Contractors are joined on their State licence number, other firms on the exact normalised name. With 1,157 firms tested, the usual one-in-twenty threshold would pass about 58 by luck alone, so the cut is set at 4.1 standard errors, where about 0.05 firms in the whole list would pass by luck. As a check on the arithmetic, the same permits dealt at random into 1,199 pretend firms put 5.8% beyond the usual threshold and 0 beyond the strict cut. Consistent means beyond that cut, on the same side of one half in at least three quarters of active years, and at least 55% (or at most 45%) overall. The last two columns repeat the comparison inside cells that also match the kind of work (keywords in the permit description) and the building's use.","limits":"Being fast is not wrongdoing. Speed has honest causes: complete drawings, repeat work in one building type, simple scopes inside a cost band, a specialty the cells do not capture (signs, fire alarms, mechanical work, office interiors), and experience. Where the difference does not survive the kind-of-work control it is most likely a specialty. The department's fee table prices a premium plan review (50% of Plan Review Fee plus $1,000), and its permit records carry a priority flag; one value of that flag is 'Premium' and the commonest others are 'ADU-State/Hybrid', 'Unit Legalization', 'Clean Energy', 'Housing-Aff'. The data dictionary does not define the values, so reading 'Premium' as the paid service is an inference. Permits are compared only with permits carrying the same kind of flag, and the share of each firm's jobs flagged Premium is shown. On 53% of full-review permits the first contractor was attached more than 60 days after filing, so a contractor's speed there may reflect its clients and designers. The records do not say whether a wait was the City's or the applicant's. Names typed differently on different permits are counted as different firms, which weakens the test and can split one firm into two rows. The file starts with 2012 filings.","tier":"REDFLAG","columns":[{"k":"firm","t":"Firm or licensed professional","type":"org"},{"k":"role","t":"Listed on permits as","type":"text"},{"k":"jobs","t":"Jobs compared","type":"int"},{"k":"years","t":"Active years","type":"text"},{"k":"yearsWith","t":"Years faster than half","type":"text"},{"k":"share","t":"Share faster than the like-permit median","type":"pct"},{"k":"days","t":"Their median days to issue","type":"days"},{"k":"like","t":"Median for like permits","type":"days"},{"k":"value","t":"Construction value on those permits","type":"usd"},{"k":"workShare","t":"Share faster after also matching the kind of work","type":"pct"},{"k":"survives","t":"Difference survives that control?","type":"text"},{"k":"premium","t":"Share of their jobs flagged 'Premium'","type":"pct"},{"k":"mostly","t":"Most common kind of work","type":"text"}],"rows":[{"firm":"E. J. Weber Elect.Co Inc","firmKey":null,"role":"Contractor","jobs":99,"years":"2015–2024","yearsWith":"8 of 8","share":0.884,"days":0,"like":10.5,"value":4867827,"workShare":0.856,"survives":"Yes","premium":0.0,"mostly":"mechanical, electrical or plumbing equipment (39%)"},{"firm":"Diseno","firmKey":null,"role":"Engineer","jobs":72,"years":"2018–2024","yearsWith":"7 of 7","share":0.864,"days":7.0,"like":27.0,"value":15886082,"workShare":0.714,"survives":"Yes","premium":0.0,"mostly":"revision or renewal of an earlier permit (34%)"},{"firm":"Ondarosa Architects","firmKey":null,"role":"Architect","jobs":54,"years":"2019–2024","yearsWith":"5 of 5","share":0.852,"days":0.5,"like":17.5,"value":5842647,"workShare":0.816,"survives":"Yes","premium":0.036,"mostly":"foundation or structural work (25%)"},{"firm":"K Lui General Construction Co.","firmKey":null,"role":"Contractor","jobs":70,"years":"2014–2018","yearsWith":"5 of 5","share":0.848,"days":1.0,"like":9.0,"value":3114904,"workShare":0.877,"survives":"Yes","premium":0.0,"mostly":"seismic or soft-story work (77%)"},{"firm":"West Coast Premier Construction Inc","firmKey":null,"role":"Contractor","jobs":135,"years":"2014–2022","yearsWith":"7 of 7","share":0.844,"days":0,"like":19,"value":12982415,"workShare":0.873,"survives":"Yes","premium":0.0,"mostly":"seismic or soft-story work (80%)"},{"firm":"Matrix Hg Inc","firmKey":null,"role":"Contractor","jobs":135,"years":"2013–2024","yearsWith":"11 of 11","share":0.838,"days":1,"like":